---
title: Cool Smarter, Not Harder
subtitle: How Fans Can Cut Your Singapore Cooling Bill
date: 2026-09-25
---
Singapore does not have a “summer season.” We have humidity, afternoon heat, and rooms that stay warm long after the sun drops. For most households, air-conditioning is the single biggest line on the electricity bill — often $67–$100 a month for one bedroom unit running overnight at today’s regulated tariff of about 34.78 cents/kWh (including GST).

Fans will not replace aircon on a 33°C afternoon. Used well, they can make you feel cooler at a fraction of the cost, and they can let you raise the aircon setpoint without losing comfort.

### Why fans work here

A fan does not lower the room temperature. It moves air across your skin so sweat evaporates faster, which is why it feels cooler. That “wind-chill” effect is useful in Singapore’s humid climate when someone is actually in the room. Leave a fan spinning in an empty room and you are only moving warm air around.

A typical DC ceiling fan on medium speed uses about 15–25W. Eight hours costs a few cents. An inverter aircon drawing 800W for the same night costs a couple of dollars. That gap is the whole strategy.

### 1. Place fans where people actually sit and sleep

Put a ceiling or standing fan over the sofa, dining table, or bed — not in a corridor. In a landed house, use two fans to create a path of air from a shaded window toward a warmer side of the house. In an HDB flat, point the fan so air travels across the room instead of blasting one person.

### 2. Run ceiling fans the right way

Most ceiling fans in Singapore should blow air **downward** in warm weather. That is the setting that creates the cooling breeze on your skin. Switch them off when you leave the room. A fan only cools people, not furniture.

### 3. Choose DC fans when you replace one

Look for **DC motor** fans and NEA energy labels. DC models typically use far less power than older AC fans at the same airflow. Climate Vouchers have also supported eligible DC fans, which makes an upgrade more practical.

### 4. Use night air when it actually helps

Singapore nights stay humid, but they are often a few degrees cooler than mid-afternoon. If outdoor air is cooler and the haze or rain is not an issue, open windows on opposite sides of the home and use a fan to pull air through. Close up again before the morning heat builds.

This works better in some landed homes and higher floors than in tightly packed blocks. If the air outside feels hotter or stickier than indoors, keep the windows shut.

### 5. Pair fans with aircon instead of fighting it

NEA’s comfort guidance of around **25°C** is the sweet spot for many homes. Run the aircon at 25°C (not 22°C) and keep a fan on while you are in the room. You feel cooler, the compressor works less, and the bill drops. Use sleep or timer modes so the unit is not cooling an empty room at 4am.

### 6. Cut heat before you try to blow it away

Close curtains or blinds on west-facing windows from mid-afternoon. Keep kitchen heat from drifting into living spaces after cooking. Clean fan blades and aircon filters; dust makes both work harder. In HDB bedrooms, a closed door while the aircon runs keeps the cooled air where you need it.

### 7. Match speed to the moment

High speed helps when you first walk into a warm room. Medium or low is usually enough once you have settled, and it uses less power. If you need the fan on high all night, the room is probably still too warm — fix the heat source or the aircon setting first.

### A simple Singapore cooling stack

1. Shade the room in the afternoon.
2. Set aircon to about 25°C only when people are home.
3. Add a fan in occupied rooms.
4. Use timers so cooling stops after you fall asleep.
5. Use fans alone on milder nights.

Generic tips help. Your home is not generic. An east-facing HDB bedroom, a west-facing landed living room, and a unit with solar plus battery will not have the same cheapest cooling pattern.

